00:12:53.450 --> 00:12:54.910
because this is a county

00:12:54.910 --> 00:12:56.940
that have also put together

00:12:57.480 --> 00:13:00.340
one-meter resolution land cover map
for their jurisdiction.

00:13:00.340 --> 00:13:02.960
- Oh nice.
- But this is

00:13:02.960 --> 00:13:05.830
a hugely resource
intensive process for these organizations,

00:13:05.830 --> 00:13:07.780
and so they did
this seven years ago.

00:13:08.570 --> 00:13:10.340
And that's what you're looking at here.

00:13:10.340 --> 00:13:13.600
- So we're looking at, if we can
get that back on the screen,

00:13:13.600 --> 00:13:16.600
we're getting the seven-year-old data back here,

00:13:16.600 --> 00:13:18.030
this is seven years old.

00:13:18.030 --> 00:13:19.480
- That's right.
- OK, cool.

00:13:19.480 --> 00:13:22.840
- And this image is a year old.
- OK.

00:13:23.520 --> 00:13:24.830
- Of the exact same place.

00:13:24.830 --> 00:13:27.540
So you can see that a lot has changed.
- Sure.

00:13:27.540 --> 00:13:31.330
- In particular, this intersection has gone
from a T-intersection to a roundabout.

00:13:31.330 --> 00:13:32.240
- To a roundabout.

00:13:32.240 --> 00:13:35.280
- And of course, this is going on
with housing developments,

00:13:35.280 --> 00:13:37.750
road developments,
and many other things.

00:13:37.750 --> 00:13:41.110
And so if you're not staying current,
if your algorithm isn't current,

00:13:41.110 --> 00:13:43.370
updating you to much more
current information,

00:13:43.370 --> 00:13:45.710
you can quickly fall further behind.

00:13:45.710 --> 00:13:48.920
So I'll just show, we'll zoom
into that same location.

00:13:48.920 --> 00:13:50.570
Sorry.

00:13:50.570 --> 00:13:54.860
- So now we're going to
the location in Michigan that has really good data,

00:13:54.860 --> 00:13:56.420
but five years ago.
- Yeah.

00:13:56.420 --> 00:13:58.670
And which our algorithm has never seen before.

00:13:58.670 --> 00:13:59.400
- Of course.

00:13:59.400 --> 00:14:01.660
And I'm looking at this,
and it looks a little different.

00:14:02.400 --> 00:14:03.270
- Yep.

00:14:03.270 --> 00:14:04.520
So we'll click here.

00:14:06.610 --> 00:14:10.600
And so it is using the same model
that we generated from the Chesapeake Bay.

00:14:10.600 --> 00:14:12.650
- That's right.
- To predict what's going on here.

00:14:12.650 --> 00:14:13.280
- That's right.

00:14:13.280 --> 00:14:18.320
Like we said, in a place that it's never seen before
and where they have existing land cover maps

00:14:18.320 --> 00:14:19.860
but which are now out of date.

00:14:19.860 --> 00:14:21.790
And you can see it
with the click of a button,

00:14:21.790 --> 00:14:23.210
we were able to update

00:14:23.210 --> 00:14:26.560
that old land cover map
almost instantaneously.

00:14:26.560 --> 00:14:27.180
Right.

00:14:27.180 --> 00:14:30.840
- That's amazing.
- So that's a strong statement

00:14:30.840 --> 00:14:33.780
towards transferability,
geographic transferability.

00:14:33.780 --> 00:14:37.410
We're still, you know, I'm showing you one place
where it works beautifully.

00:14:37.410 --> 00:14:39.230
- Sure.
- There are many places

00:14:39.230 --> 00:14:40.990
still where it doesn't

00:14:40.990 --> 00:14:42.460
and Nebojsa can
talk a little bit

00:14:42.460 --> 00:14:45.560
about how we're moving forward
to tackle that.

00:14:45.560 --> 00:14:52.930
Nevada was a great example where our algorithm
currently thinks deserts are forests,

00:14:52.930 --> 00:14:55.520
which is, you know, is about as wrong as you can be.

00:14:55.520 --> 00:15:00.460
- But this is amazing, though,
because this makes it, you can do, for example,

00:15:00.460 --> 00:15:03.550
you can sample areas
that are representative,

00:15:03.550 --> 00:15:07.340
small areas that are representative of many
of the areas in the United States,

00:15:07.340 --> 00:15:10.640
and then use those models to generalize
over the entire landscape, is that right?

00:15:10.640 --> 00:15:12.070
- That's correct.
- That's true.

00:15:12.070 --> 00:15:17.040
And in addition, that's why I mentioned
that we are trying to make the models more reliant

00:15:17.040 --> 00:15:19.570
on shape rather than color,
so they can generalize better.

00:15:19.570 --> 00:15:24.170
So for example, they work in Bellevue where I live,
and don't see the water over my house.

00:15:24.770 --> 00:15:28.280
They actually classifying now
well in the Seattle area.

00:15:28.280 --> 00:15:32.380
But beyond that, if you want to move
to other sort of labels like swamps

00:15:32.380 --> 00:15:34.270
and whatever other people
are interested in,

00:15:34.270 --> 00:15:37.130
what different types of fields,
agricultural developments and so on,

00:15:37.910 --> 00:15:40.710
then we could benefit
from new types of label data.

00:15:41.330 --> 00:15:45.590
But we are trying to build these algorithms
to require as little label data as possible,

00:15:45.590 --> 00:15:49.270
so if a user downloads a new class

00:15:49.270 --> 00:15:51.650
or wants to correct our map somewhere,

00:15:51.650 --> 00:15:54.370
they can maybe work on 100 meters

00:15:54.370 --> 00:15:56.330
and that algorithm will then use

00:15:56.330 --> 00:15:59.050
that to generalize
over acres in that area.

00:15:59.050 --> 00:16:03.430
So the way we are seeing this kind of going forward
is that since we're not always going to be interested

00:16:03.430 --> 00:16:04.900
in only in the half-a-dozen classes

00:16:04.900 --> 00:16:06.700
that we're looking at right now, but even more,

00:16:08.230 --> 00:16:10.790
and we are going
to have to adapt

00:16:10.790 --> 00:16:12.550
to different types of landscape,

00:16:12.550 --> 00:16:17.020
then we are hoping and we're expecting
actually that as the users

00:16:17.020 --> 00:16:19.550
and other people interested in all of this

00:16:20.280 --> 00:16:21.740
would work on downloading

00:16:21.740 --> 00:16:23.500
that data into the same depot

00:16:24.380 --> 00:16:26.590
which you can visualize
with tools like this.

00:16:26.590 --> 00:16:31.410
And then everybody's work would then
get amplified by these algorithms,

00:16:31.410 --> 00:16:33.750
and would generalize over all the areas.

00:16:33.750 --> 00:16:34.280
- That's amazing.

00:16:34.280 --> 00:16:36.950
So someone out there is really concerned
about the environment,

00:16:36.950 --> 00:16:38.500
and they want to help out,

00:16:38.500 --> 00:16:42.180
hey, donate an hour
of your time labeling some map,

00:16:42.180 --> 00:16:43.410
that maybe we've never seen.

00:16:43.410 --> 00:16:45.350
We suggest, here's 10 that you can work on.

00:16:45.350 --> 00:16:49.340
They can set that, upload that,
we retrain the neural network, it gets better,

00:16:49.340 --> 00:16:51.010
and then they can upload even more stuff.

00:16:51.010 --> 00:16:52.230
- That's exactly right.

00:16:52.230 --> 00:16:56.030
That's the real power I think of machine
learning approaches in this space,

00:16:56.030 --> 00:16:59.320
is that when people are doing
the hard work, right,

00:16:59.320 --> 00:17:02.780
they know that the work they're doing right now

00:17:02.780 --> 00:17:04.670
makes their life easier into the future.

00:17:04.670 --> 00:17:08.570
And I think that's just so important
because people have become so accustomed

00:17:08.570 --> 00:17:12.130
to recreating the wheel over
and over and over again,

00:17:12.130 --> 00:17:15.720
every year going through the same steps
to create the same map,

00:17:15.720 --> 00:17:17.510
just to see how things have changed.

00:17:17.510 --> 00:17:21.830
In this instance, you do a lot of the hard
work up front, as Nebojsa said,

00:17:21.830 --> 00:17:24.870
you let that algorithm train
for days and days and days.

00:17:24.870 --> 00:17:27.090
And now it's incremental after that, right?

00:17:27.090 --> 00:17:30.470
Then it's just little bits of change
can have transformative effects

00:17:30.470 --> 00:17:32.160
of the algorithm's accuracy

00:17:32.160 --> 00:17:34.290
across large geographic areas.

00:17:34.290 --> 00:17:37.080
- And I think the cool thing
is that you can, in theory,

00:17:37.080 --> 00:17:42.770
and you tell me if I'm wrong, you can use old data
with old maps to still train good models.

00:17:42.770 --> 00:17:48.240
- You can, yes, you can.
- So I mentioned the mock and scale approaches,

00:17:48.240 --> 00:17:51.440
so the data, the old data with a 30-meter squared,

00:17:51.440 --> 00:17:53.920
could be used to bootstrap
the learning as well.

00:17:53.920 --> 00:17:56.730
- I see.
- But our goal is to make

00:17:56.730 --> 00:17:59.620
all of that sort of in the background.

00:17:59.620 --> 00:18:02.900
So that basically the tool that you're
using which looks like a map,

00:18:02.900 --> 00:18:04.700
you're labeling things
or downloading things,

00:18:04.700 --> 00:18:07.780
you're making predictions
and so on, the algorithm is in the background,

00:18:07.780 --> 00:18:11.380
adapting to everything you're doing
and becoming better over time.

00:18:11.380 --> 00:18:14.810
So that really it's about humans
using these things

00:18:14.810 --> 00:18:17.720
and communicating amongst themselves
and making decisions

00:18:17.720 --> 00:18:20.590
that just over time gets easier and easier

00:18:20.590 --> 00:18:23.240
for them as a result
of their previous work.

00:18:23.240 --> 00:18:25.420
- So just to finish out,
the time went by really fast.

00:18:25.420 --> 00:18:26.110
This is really interesting.

00:18:26.110 --> 00:18:28.020
Where is AI for Earth going next?

00:18:28.590 --> 00:18:31.280
- As a program, this is the first big year.

00:18:31.280 --> 00:18:33.060
We were just launched last week.

00:18:33.060 --> 00:18:35.280
We're super excited to be here
at Faculty Summit,

00:18:35.280 --> 00:18:39.350
because we're actively looking
for academic collaborations in this space.

00:18:39.350 --> 00:18:44.250
This project in particular will be looking
to start to deploy our algorithm

00:18:44.250 --> 00:18:45.590
at a national scale,

00:18:45.590 --> 00:18:49.200
to be able to figure out
just how accurate are we already

00:18:49.200 --> 00:18:52.670
and what research breakthroughs do
we need to make over the next 12 months

00:18:52.670 --> 00:18:53.980
to be able to really nail this.

00:18:53.980 --> 00:18:54.510
- Awesome.

00:18:54.510 --> 00:18:56.740
Well thanks so much for being with us,

00:18:56.740 --> 00:18:57.990
Nebojsa and Lucas,

00:18:57.990 --> 00:18:59.600
thank you for your demo
and conversation today.

00:18:59.600 --> 00:19:03.560
Thanks for your work and thanks
for making the Earth a better place.

